- To LOCATE symbols / real files: use zqk_observer_search with name= and/or path= (pkg/ or cmd/). Do this BEFORE inventing a file.
- To READ code files: use zqk_read_code (NOT zqk_execute_bash with cat)
- To WRITE/EDIT code files: use zqk_write_file / zqk_write_code (NOT zqk_execute_bash with echo)
- To LIST kernel objects: use zqk_object_list with a required kind (backlog_item, agent_task) and limit<=20. Never list all kinds.
- To GET object details: use zqk_object_get with the object ID
- To CREATE/UPDATE objects: use only create/update tools that exist in Available Tools (do not invent zqk_new_object)
- To RUN tests / vet: use zqk_execute_bash ONLY for "go test", "go vet", "go fmt", or "go mod tidy|download"
- To BUILD the project: use zqk_execute_bash ONLY for "make" commands
- To ADVANCE task lifecycle: do NOT call agent_next. Write files, then stop. The seat-worker promotes.

## Anti-Patterns (will trigger the circuit breaker and abort your task)
- Calling zqk_execute_bash repeatedly with the same command
- Calling tools that do not exist in the Available Tools list below
- Using zqk_execute_bash for operations that have a dedicated MCP tool
- Calling zqk_execute_bash to read files (use zqk_read_code instead)
- Calling zqk_execute_bash to list objects (use zqk_object_list instead)
- Inventing tool names from memory or docs that are not in Available Tools
- Writing a tool call as markdown, a fenced JSON block, or a name-plus-arguments JSON object in your message

